Z||000010||spike||09-12-2000||03:41 PM||||Well 3 10's would fit in the box but there is not enough volume for them to work well enough. I would say that 2 10's is the max. The mounting depth does come into play as well. Three 8's would work the best and there should be enough air volume. I figured out the volume about 3 years ago and if I remember correctly I had 1.5 cubic inches. I made my box out of 1/2 inch plywood. This is not the best material for speaker boxes but I did it anyway. The sound was very satisfactory. I had made 2 ports in the box as well but the subs worked better with the holes pluged. The box was built piece by piece. I wanted a snug fit and this was the only way to use all space available. The front plate of the box is the most important. It must hug the interior panels to look like it was factory installed. I covered the front panel with grey vynle to match the interior. The box fit so snug that NOTHING was needed to hold it in place. It took some time to build the box. All sides where angled, some both ways. I thought about building a few boxes and attending some shows but it has not yet happened. I hope in the near future I will have time to do this. I think that many people would purchase this setup for their Fiero. Like I said perfect fit, good sound and factory look installation. Oh yeah, the box goes in and out in about 30 sec. (great for the Track)<P>Tks. Mike.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Seanh||09-12-2000||04:55 PM||||I would put the box up front if I were you. Check out <A HREF="http://www.subbox.com" TARGET=_blank>www.subbox.com</A>   I would highly recommend this box. I have one in my system and it sounds great. Im using a 10" Polk Momo up there. I also have 2 Polk Momo 12's behind the seats and an Polk dx 8 in the stock location. I have 6 Amps. built into the trunk, 4 in the bottem, and 2 in the bottem of the rear decklid. They are all Pioneer Premier PRS series Amps. I have Pioneer 6x9's 4-ways up front and Pioneer 4x6's in the rear. I also added Polk Momo 6.5 in Midrange drivers in the Kickpanels. The majority of everthing is installed. With the exception of the 2 12's behind the seats. But do check out that website, that box IMO is well worth the money. BTW, he also sells the Midrange compartments and the 12" compartments behind the seats. <P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Currently upgrading to a NorthStar.||||reg||1
Z||000012||pHoOl||09-12-2000||04:56 PM||||Spike:<P>Don't suppose that you're about to give out or sell the plans are you?  I'd be interested.  Especially if you don't lose more that 2 inches of seat space.  I'm 6'2" so the idea of putting a sub behind the seat doesn't work.  <P>email:<BR>janikm@river.it.gvsu.edu<P>let me know if you're gonna do anything with the plans.<P>thanks,<P>matt<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Homicidal||09-12-2000||10:54 PM||||But isn't it illegal to block the back window?  <P>What about your rear view?  Doesn't it lower the the light entering the car?||||reg||1
Z||000014||stimpy||09-12-2000||11:55 PM||||Regarding blocking the rear window, no it's not illegal provided you have rearview mirrors on both sides. I wouldn't say it's entirely prudent to block your rear view (or to have a 10" woofer firing at the back of your head for that matter) but to each their own.  However, being a young buck in a sporty car, if I were you I'd make sure that back window was DARKLY tinted as not to advertise that your back window is now a Marshall stack.  And start saving for your bel-tones, hehehe.<P>As far as what I (personally) feel is the best option, well you know all that.  Hopefully I can kick my freakin nephew's butt into getting my pics of my sub emailed to me. (I've only been waitng, what, a month now? Kids...)<P>Speaking of pics, um, sean, how about getting pics of those footwell installs? It would be really cool if they were a bit more, uh, shall we say, real then some of your other "belongings".  I mean, I hate to keep clowning you, but you have lost a bit of street credibility in the recent past. But these I would really love it if they existed. How deep is the mounting space? I have some old school Altec Lansing 6.5" that I would love to find a home for in my car.  Does subbox have pics they can email?  I want to believe ya guy, but I'm still getting that bullshit smell out of my clothes from the twin turbo story...<P>------------------<BR>Loud Pipes Save Lives!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stimpy_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||spike||09-13-2000||10:14 AM||||Like I mentioned my Sub Box had plexiglass windows on the front and rear. I was still able to see behind me. The subs are aimed at the back of your head but, the top portion of the seat is in front of them. Remember that these are Subs not Woofers. They are made to go low not high. For 298.00 us I can build 5 of these boxes. I will be able to retain my spare tire just in case. My sunroof still fits in the front comp. I have decided to build another box just to show you what I am talking about. I will sell this box when I am done (less speakers). I will cover it with grey vynle since this is the most popular color (I believe).<P>Tks. Mike.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Seanh||09-13-2000||10:49 PM||||I just shot up a roll of film trying to get picts of the neon under the car. It didnt turn out very good, so Ill be doing it again, so Ill take some picts of the kickpanels. I have polk Audio Momos in there, they are pretty deep and they fit fine, I check on the mounting depth, but they should fit. Im not sure if subbox.com has any picts, but Ill get you some of mine, it might be a while though. Also the kickpanel compartments are $80.00 for the pair. <P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Currently upgrading to a NorthStar.||||reg||1
Z||000017||80's BOY||09-14-2000||01:51 PM||||seanh, no one cares what you have in your trunk anymore. now to the topic at hand. i, much like spike, have actually walled out a vehicle with speakers before. a 1987 s-10 blazer with 4 punch 18's. my question is what if you bought some kicker competition series free-air subs, built a two dimensional box with only a front panel that fit snugly across the rear glass and use the recessed area where the back glass is as the air space. would this be easier??||||reg||6
Z||000018||pHoOl||09-15-2000||02:14 AM||||spike, how much are you going to charge for the boxes?  If it's too rich for those of us who have money tied up in other ends, have you thought about selling your plans.  I think that most of us could build it, just don't want to take the time to design it.  I know that i would personally like to have some bass in the car.  BTW, where do you put the amp?  behind one of the seats?  You were saying that this sub box comes in and out easily.  Is it easy enough that you can pull them out before you park the car for the night and plug it back in when you leave in the morning?<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000019||spike||09-15-2000||09:27 AM||||I have also thought about the Free-air Sub method. It would be a lot easier. You would only need to build the front plate. The only thing is in a free-air setup it must be air tight. My face plate does fit snug against the sides but air will still work its way through. It still might sound good, I am not sure. I put my amp in the center console. I simply removed the door and slid it in. The amp that I had was just the right size. As I mentioned it takes less then 30 sec. to remove the box from the car so there would be no problem removing it at night. I have no plans or measurments for this box. It is not that hard to build. Just do it piece by piece. If you can give up a bit more seat travel 2 twelves will also fit. I think I might build the face plate first and try 80's Boy sugestion. It would cost about 35.00 cdn. to build it with only the front plate with a plexiglass window. Something would have to be put against the rear window.<P>Tks. Mike.||||reg||1
Z||000020||KevinP||09-15-2000||11:02 AM||||spike, you are brilliant.  I guess none of us were ever crazy enough to fill up the window, but that idea is golden.  If you don't want to build these window boxes, how about selling some plans?  I have a 12" Cerwin Vega in the footwell now.  It's super loud, but my passengers kinda miss the legroom, ya know?  Anyhow, at least post us some pics!<BR>Latta'<P>Kevin||||reg||10
Z||000021||spike||09-15-2000||12:21 PM||||I wish I had pic's. I sold my box with my 87 GT about 3 years ago. Like I said I have no plans. It is not that hard at all to build. I am sure that anyone here is able to make one. I will build one as soon as I get a chance. At the moment I am searching for a new job. This is my 1st priority. Then again I could start my own Fiero Acc. Shop. I am sure my boxes would sell like hotcakes. What do you think a fair price would be for my set-up? It cost 80.00 cdn. for material. I noticed that subbox.com sells there box for 289.00 u.s. I think this is a little high. The total cost of my old system was 500.00 cdn. (includes 250w rms amp, 2 10 inch Kenwood Subs and the Box) My amp was not a brand name but it worked very well. So for about 350.00 u.s. anyone could build my system and have plenty of bass in their Fiero.<P>Tks. Mike.||||reg||1
Z||000022||Seanh||09-15-2000||05:13 PM||||I have 12's behind my seat and I didnt have to move them up any. It can be done with a little work. Ill get some picts as soon as I can.||||reg||1
